# The complete intersection criterion at a prime

Let `(A, 𝔪)` be a Noetherian local Cohen-Macaulay ring and let `I ⊆ A` be a proper ideal whose
quotient has codimension `c`, that is `dim (A/I) + c = dim A`. Then the following are equivalent:
`I` can be generated by `c` elements; the conormal module `I/I²` can be generated by `c` elements
over `A/I`; `I/I²` is a free `A/I`-module of rank `c`; and `I` can be generated by a regular
sequence. Moreover any `c` elements of `I` generating `I/𝔪I` form a regular sequence.

Applied to `A = k[x_i]_{𝔮'}`, the localization at `𝔮'` of a polynomial ring in finitely many
variables over a field — a regular, hence Cohen-Macaulay, local ring of dimension `height 𝔮'` —
this gives the equivalence of items (2), (3), (4) and (5) of
`Algebra.Generators.tfae_isGlobalCompleteIntersection_away`, together with the closing assertion
`Algebra.Generators.spanFinrank_ker_map_le_iff_exists_isRegular`. Item (1), that `S_g` is a
global complete intersection over `k` for some `g ∉ 𝔮`, joins them: it follows from item (2) by
an explicit construction, and it implies item (2) through the stable isomorphism of naive
cotangent complexes, read over the residue field `κ(𝔮)`.

## Main results

* `Ideal.spanFinrank_top_cotangent_eq_spanFinrank`: over a Noetherian local ring, the minimal
  number of generators of a proper ideal `I` equals the minimal number of generators of the
  conormal module `I/I²` as an `A/I`-module. This is Nakayama's lemma, `I²  ⊆ 𝔪I` making the two
  reductions agree.
* `Ideal.free_and_finrank_cotangent_of_isWeaklyRegular`: if `rs` is a weakly regular sequence
  generating a proper ideal `J`, then `J/J²` is a free `A/J`-module of rank `rs.length`.
* `Module.IsMaximalCohenMacaulay.isRegular_of_ofList_eq`: in a Cohen-Macaulay local ring, a list of
  `c` elements generating an ideal of codimension `c` is a regular sequence.
* `RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular.length_eq_of_ofList_eq`: conversely, a regular sequence generating
  an ideal of codimension `c` has length exactly `c`.
* `Module.IsMaximalCohenMacaulay.tfae_spanFinrank_cotangent`: the four equivalent conditions.
* `Module.IsMaximalCohenMacaulay.isRegular_of_sup_mul_eq`: the closing assertion, that any `c`
  elements of `I` generating `I/𝔪I` form a regular sequence.
* `ringKrullDim_quotient_map_ker_eq_height`: for a surjection `f : R → T`, a prime `𝔮 ⊆ T` and a
  localization `A` of `R` at `𝔮' = f⁻¹(𝔮)`, the ring `A/(ker f)A` is a localization of `T` at `𝔮`,
  so its Krull dimension is `height 𝔮`.
* `Algebra.Generators.spanFinrank_ker_map_le_iff_exists_isRegular`: items (2) and (5), together
  with the closing assertion.
* `PrimeSpectrum.exists_notMem_topologicalKrullDim_basicOpen_le_localKrullDim`: the infimum
  defining the dimension of a spectrum at a point is attained on a standard open `D(g)`.
* `PrimeSpectrum.localKrullDim_add_height_comap_eq_natCard_add_height`: for a presentation
  `S = k[x_i]/I` in `n` variables, `dim_q Spec S + height q' = n + height q`.
* `mvpoly_quotient_span_range_ringKrullDim_ge`: a nonzero quotient of `k[x_1, …, x_N]` by `C`
  elements has dimension at least `N - C`.
* `Algebra.Generators.exists_isGlobalCompleteIntersection_away_of_spanFinrank_ker_map_le`: the
  implication (2) ⇒ (1), and
  `Algebra.Generators.exists_isGlobalCompleteIntersection_away_of_exists_isRegular`, the
  implication (5) ⇒ (1).
* `PrimeSpectrum.topologicalKrullDim_basicOpen_le_ringKrullDim` and
  `PrimeSpectrum.localKrullDim_le_ringKrullDim_of_notMem`: a standard open `D(g)` of a spectrum
  has the dimension of `R[g⁻¹]`, so the dimension of `Spec R` at a point of `D(g)` is at most
  `dim R[g⁻¹]`.
* `Algebra.Generators.spanFinrank_ker_map_le_of_exists_isGlobalCompleteIntersection_away`: the
  implication (1) ⇒ (2).
* `Algebra.Generators.tfae_isGlobalCompleteIntersection_away`: the five equivalent conditions.

## Implementation notes

*The codimension hypothesis is the equation `dim (A/I) + c = dim A` in `WithBot ℕ∞`, not
`dim (A/I) = dim A - c`.* Krull dimensions live in `WithBot ℕ∞`, where subtraction is truncated and
would make the hypothesis strictly weaker; the additive form also forces `A/I` to be nonzero, since
`⊥ + c = ⊥`. It is the form Mathlib states the dimension of a quotient by a regular sequence in
(`ringKrullDim_add_length_eq_ringKrullDim_of_isRegular`).

*"Can be generated by `c` elements" is `Submodule.spanFinrank ≤ c`,* Mathlib's minimal number of
generators, the invariant its Krull height theory is phrased with. Krull's height theorem in the
form `ringKrullDim_le_ringKrullDim_quotient_add_spanFinrank` is what forces that number to be at
least `c`, so a generating set of size at most `c` has size exactly `c` and the dimension of the
quotient drops by exactly `c`.

*The conormal module is Mathlib's `Ideal.Cotangent`,* that is `I ⧸ (I • ⊤ : Submodule A I)`, an
`A/I`-module; `Algebra.Extension.Cotangent` of a presentation is a type synonym for it.

*Freeness of the conormal module is proved directly.* A relation `∑ aᵢ rᵢ = 0` among a weakly
regular sequence has all `aᵢ` in the ideal generated by the sequence
(`mem_ofList_of_sum_eq_zero`, an induction peeling the last element of the sequence), and this
gives the basis.

*The conormal items (3) and (4) are stated for the ideal `I_A = I_{𝔮'}` of
`A = k[x_i]_{𝔮'}`, not for `(I/I²)_𝔮` over `S_𝔮`.* The two agree — `A/I_A` is `S_𝔮`, which is
`ringKrullDim_quotient_map_ker_eq_height`, and `I_A/I_A²` is `(I/I²)_𝔮` because localization is
flat — and the form used here is the one the Cohen-Macaulay criterion is proved in.

*The implication (1) ⇒ (2) is proved over the residue field `κ(𝔮)`, not over `S_𝔮`.*
`Algebra.Generators.nonempty_cotangent_baseChange_prod_linearEquiv_of_isLocalizationAway` gives
an isomorphism `(I/I²)_g ⊕ S_g^{⊕m} ≅ J/J² ⊕ S_g^{⊕n}` of `S_g`-modules for a second presentation
`S_g = k[y_1, …, y_m]/J`, from which the rank of `(I/I²)_𝔮` is read off after cancelling the free
summands. Base changing to `κ(𝔮)` makes both sides finite dimensional `κ`-vector spaces, where
the dimension is additive, so the free summands cancel numerically
(`finrank_add_eq_of_prodPi_linearEquiv`). The two ends meet at Nakayama's lemma, which says that
the minimal number of generators of `I_A` is `dim_κ (κ ⊗ I_A)`
(`spanFinrank_map_le_finrank_residueField_tensor`, one half of Mathlib's
`TensorProduct.spanFinrank_top_eq_of_residueField`).

*`Spec S_g` is identified with `D(g) ⊆ Spec S` as a topological space, not just mapped into it.*
The numerical input to (1) ⇒ (2) is `dim_𝔮 Spec S ≤ dim S_g`, and `dim_𝔮 Spec S` is an infimum of
`topologicalKrullDim U` over open `U ∋ 𝔮`, so it needs `topologicalKrullDim D(g) ≤ dim S_g` — the
inequality opposite to `PrimeSpectrum.ringKrullDim_le_topologicalKrullDim_basicOpen`. The
corestriction `Spec S_g → D(g)` is an inducing bijection, hence a homeomorphism, which gives both.

